<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mul Mantar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Mul Mantar is the opening verse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> scripture, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-granth-sahib">Guru Granth Sahib</a>, a twelve-word statement in Punjabi in the Gurmukhi script. It is the best known of Sikh texts and summarises the teaching of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-nanak">Guru Nanak</a>. After Ik Onkar, its words literally mean true name, the creator, without fear, without hate, timeless in form, beyond birth, self-existent, known by the grace of the Guru. Translators differ over the first two words, rendering them &quot;There is one god&quot; or &quot;One reality is&quot;. The verse is repeated before many hymns. It existed in several versions in the 16th century, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-arjan">Guru Arjan</a> gave it its final form in the 17th.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mul-mantar.jpg" alt="Mul Mantar" width="330" height="270" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ms Sarah Welch · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:17th_century_Mul_Mantar,_Guru_Arjan_handwriting,_Kartarpur_manuscript.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The Mul Mantar consists of twelve words in Punjabi in the Gurmukhi script and is the most widely known part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> scripture. A mantar is a succinct sacred formula, and mul means &quot;root&quot; or &quot;fundamental&quot;, so the name means &quot;root formula&quot;. The verse is repeated before many hymns, or shabad, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-granth-sahib">Guru Granth Sahib</a> and is described as a creedal statement that symbolically describes the experience of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-nanak">Guru Nanak</a>. Its essential elements are found in his compositions, in the various epithets he used for Akal Purakh, the Ultimate Reality.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The modern text is the edited version canonised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-arjan">Guru Arjan</a> during the compiling of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-granth-sahib">Adi Granth</a>, and it differs from the one first written by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-nanak">Guru Nanak</a>. The earliest form is found in the Goindwal pothi, from the time of Guru Amar Das, which has seven variations and belongs to the Mohan Sikh tradition. Other variants come from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meena">Mina</a> scribal tradition and from the GNDU MS1245 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a>. An earlier draft of Guru Arjan's editing still read sat-gur(a)-prasad(i) where the modern text has gur(a)-prasad(i).</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The verse has posed difficulties for translators, above all its first two words, Ik Onkar, rendered as &quot;There is one god&quot;, &quot;One reality is&quot; and &quot;This being is one&quot;. Whether &quot;god&quot;, &quot;reality&quot; or &quot;being&quot; is capitalised changes the sense in English. The general view favours a monotheistic reading, though not the Abrahamic kind: it is described as &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-nanak">Guru Nanak</a>'s mystical awareness of the one that is expressed through the many&quot;. Some translations wrongly turn a list of qualities into statements about the divine.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> institutions disagree over where the verse ends. The SGPC treats it as ending at gura prasadi, holding that what follows is the name and first line of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japji-sahib">Japji Sahib</a> composition, as the number of times the shorter form appears before hymns shows. Some taksals and gurmat schools, and the Nihangs, hold the full verse to include the Jap lines and say it has been used so in the Amrit Sanchar baptising ceremony since its beginning.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mul Mantar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The scripture opens with the words “Ik Onkar”, “there is one God”, the beginning of the <b>Mul Mantar</b>, the Sikh statement of faith.</blockquote><a href="/religion/sikhism">Sikhism · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>An introductory section holds the <b>Mul Mantar</b>, Japji Sahib, So Dhar (Rehras) and Sohila, composed by Guru Nanak.</blockquote><a href="/work/guru-granth-sahib">Guru Granth Sahib · What It Is</a></li></ul></section>
